WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.220
The creatures in The Witcher might've come
from myths and legends, but they don't look

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:06.370
exactly like their traditional counterparts.

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:07.960
They don't act quite like them either.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.380
These are the stories that inspired some of
The Witcher's most fearsome beasts.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:17.040
Netflix's The Witcher opens with a swampy
duel between Geralt of Rivia and the kikimora,

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.080
a hulking spider-like creature with an unsettlingly
human head.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:24.560
While Geralt makes quick work of the beast,
the kikimora ends up getting the last laugh.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:28.500
Despite the kikimora's ferocity, no one is
willing to pay Geralt for its corpse, making

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.640
all his effort futile.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.380
While the kikimora battle makes for a great
opening set piece, The Witcher's version of

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:37.440
the creature, both in the series and in the
games, is a very liberal take on the kikimora

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:37.940
legend.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:42.220
In Slavic mythology, a kikimora is a house
spirit that sneaks into your home via your

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:44.650
keyhole in order to deliver bad news.

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:48.820
Once a kikimora settles in, it can also cause
nightmares, sleep paralysis, and nighttime

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.200
calamities like dying cattle and spoiled food.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.180
Don't ever look directly at a kikimora, either
do so, and you might die.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.140
Instead of a multi-legged monstrosity, a kikimora
typically looks like a mix between an old

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:04.190
woman and a bird, complete with a beak and
chicken legs and a headscarf wrapped around

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:05.190
her stringy hair.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.930
Unlike Geralt, you typically don't kill them
with a sword, either.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.660
Just leave some food behind the stove, where
kikimory live.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.880
According to some legends, that'll do the
trick.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.240
The striga is Geralt's foe in episode three
of the series, "Betrayer Moon," and "The Witcher,"

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.280
Andrzej Sapkowski's very first witcher story.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.410
On Netflix's The Witcher, the striga looks
like a giant humanoid monster, complete with

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:25.900
a long, dangling umbilical cord.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:38.300
In Polish folklore, the "strzyga" is no less
dangerous, but it does work a little differently.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:42.210
Strzygi were said to be people born with two
souls, and who could be identified by their

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.080
twin hearts or extra pairs of teeth.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.680
When a baby was born that people suspected
was a strzyga, they were banished.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:51.500
Then, one of their souls died and they became
monsters, often with feathers and blue or

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:55.040
grey skin, who transformed into owls to suck
the blood of their victims.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.030
The strzyga is one of the most dangerous creatures
in Polish folklore, and it's not easy to get

00:01:59.030 --> 00:01:59.520
rid of them.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:03.240
Some people claim that cutting off a strzyga's
head and burying it far away from the body

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:07.160
will get the job done, although burying one
face-down with a sickle around its neck is

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.180
said to work, too.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:12.700
The Witcher's fourth episode, "Of Banquets,
Bastards and Burials," is based on the Brothers

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.420
Grimm story "Hans My Hedgehog."

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:18.480
In that story, a child named Hans is born
with the legs of a human, but the body and

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:19.280
head of a hedgehog.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.920
Years after his family casts him out, Hans
runs across two lost kings in the forest.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:28.290
Hans offers to help them find their way, but
for a price: each ruler needs to give Hans

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:31.820
"the first thing that greets [the king] at
the royal court upon his arrival home."

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.820
That ends up being the princess, meaning Hans
gets a beautiful wife for his trouble.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.450
Hans and Duny, the cursed knight in The Witcher,
differ in a few subtle ways.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:43.090
While Duny is as big as a man, Hans is more
hedgehog-sized.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:46.080
He rides a rooster like a horse, and is often
mistaken for an animal.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.380
Further, Duny simply transforms into a human.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:53.160
Hans' shift is a lot more gruesome: not only
does he physically shed his hedgehog skin,

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:57.349
but after throwing it into the fire, his new,
human skin is charred black, requiring the

00:02:57.349 --> 00:02:58.400
aid of the local healer.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:01.620
The dryads of Brokilon Forest don't mess around.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:06.080
Forced into hiding by humans and monsters,
they fearsomely protect their land from intruders.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:09.250
They shoot Ciri's friend, Dara, before he
can even reach the woods.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:13.270
They force all newcomers to drink the water
of Brokilon, which kills anyone who harbors

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:17.400
ill-will against the forest, and makes anyone
who doesn't slowly forget their past.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.360
Accordingly, The Witcher's dryads dress in
armor and heavy makeup.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:24.120
They're imposing figures, as they should be,
and they resolutely stand their ground.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.040
That's a big departure from the dryads of
Greek mythology, where the creatures originated.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.380
In the original myths, dryads or nymphs who
were tied to forests or trees were actually

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:34.000
known for being exceptionally shy.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.920
They didn't really fight, although some of
them could transform from human-like creatures

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:38.580
into trees.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.760
For the most part, dryads are passive figures
in the Greek myths.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:43.680
It was up to The Witcher to give them some
grit.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.740
"Please, don't hurt me!"

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.260
In "Rare Species," Geralt and Jaskier join
a group of warriors who are competing to see

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:54.740
who can kill a dragon first.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:58.390
Not only is the hunt that ensues is full of
twists and turns, but it's a big part of The

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:01.890
Witcher's behind-the-scenes history: The story
that the episode is based on, "The Bounds

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:06.040
of Reason," is an adaptation of the Polish
folktale that inspired Sapkowski to create

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:07.300
Geralt in the first place.

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:12.120
The story of the Wawel Dragon dates back to
the 13th century, and while it takes many

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:13.920
forms, some elements are always the same.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:18.320
There's always a king whose subjects are terrorized
by a man-eating, cattle-devouring dragon.

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:21.560
There are always brave knights who try to
conquer the dragon in hopes of winning the

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:22.480
princess' hand.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:23.700
They always fail.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:28.059
Finally, there's always a hero, usually depicted
as a cobbler, who decides to use the dragon's

00:04:28.059 --> 00:04:29.430
own appetite against him.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:33.420
The cobbler fills a sheepskin with hay and
sulfur, then waits for the dragon to eat it.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:37.800
That makes the dragon so thirsty that it drinks
water until it literally explodes.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:41.760
It's one of Poland's most beloved stories
there's even a seven-headed statue commemorating

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:45.080
the dragon in Krakow but Sapkowski thought
it was ridiculous.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:47.680
You wouldn't call a cobbler to kill a dragon,
he figured.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:48.720
You'd call a professional.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:53.360
And so, the author invented a fictional monster
hunter, and lo and behold, Geralt of Rivia

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:53.980
was born.

